            <ArticleTitle>Low level lasers in dentistry.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>629-34</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Low level laser therapy (LLLT) uses light energy, in the form of adenosine triphosphate (ATP), to elicit biological responses in the body. The increased cellular energy and changes in the cell membrane permeability result in pain relief, wound healing, muscle relaxation, immune system modulation, and nerve regeneration. This article investigates the clinical effects of LLLT and explains how it can be applied in the dental field.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
